<section class="ss-c-demo__section">
    <h2>Best Practices</h2>

    <div class="ss-l-grid ss-l-grid-auto">
        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✓ Provide @2x assets minimum</h3>
            <p>Most modern devices are at least 2× density. Always provide @2x images; @3x is recommended for icons and
                logos.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✓ Use vector graphics when possible</h3>
            <p>SVG and icon fonts scale perfectly at any density. Reserve bitmap images for photos and complex graphics.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✓ Test on multiple densities</h3>
            <p>Use browser DevTools to simulate different DPRs. Chrome: Device Toolbar → DPR dropdown.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✓ Use image_density for backgrounds</h3>
            <p>The <code>image_density</code> mixin handles fallbacks and browser prefixes automatically.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✗ Don't assume 1× baseline</h3>
            <p>Standard 1× displays are increasingly rare. Design for 2× first, then optimize for other densities.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ss-c-card">
            <h3 class="ss-t-h4">✗ Avoid density-dependent sizing</h3>
            <p>Let CSS pixels and Q units handle scaling. Only use physical pixel calculations for specific effects like
                hairline borders.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
